The '''IG-series''' was a series of [[droid]]s manufactured by [[Holowan Laboratories]]<ref name="Ultimate Star Wars" /> during and after the [[Clone Wars]]. By the time of the [[New Republic]], IG [[memory circuit]]s were no longer manufactured and were hard to find.<ref name="Chapter 18">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 18: The Mines of Mandalore}}</ref> The [[InterGalactic Banking Clan]] maintained an [[IG-series battle droid army|army]] of [[IG lancer droid]]s that it shared with the [[Confederacy of Independent Systems]],<ref name="Rise" /> eventually fielding them to the [[Separatist Droid Army]]<ref name="Scum and Villany">''[[Star Wars: Scum and Villainy: Case Files on the Galaxy's Most Notorious]]''</ref> which field-tested them during the conflict and found them successful. These successes led Holowan to be also awarded a contract to build the [[IG-100 MagnaGuard]] as elite bodyguards for the Separatists' highest ranking officers.<ref name="Ultimate Star Wars" /> Shortly after the war, Holowan Laboratories was inspired by their droids' wartime success to begin development on even more advanced IG units,<ref name="Scene to Stats IG-Series">{{AtomicMassGames|url=transmission/scene-to-stats-ig-series-assassin-droid|text=Scene to Stats: IG-Series Assassin Droid|date=2022-9-20}}</ref> including the [[IG-RM bodyguard and enforcer droid]]s.<ref name="Character enyclopedia" />

One of these new [[IG-88 assassin droid|models]] was the [[IG-88 assassin droid]], a set of five identical [[assassin droid]]s who [[Holowan Laboratories massacre|slaughtered]] their constructors moments after their activation and escaped their [[Unidentified laboratories (Holowan Laboratories)|laboratories]] to roam [[the galaxy]].<ref name="CVD">''[[Star Wars: The Complete Visual Dictionary, New Edition]]''</ref> The new IG-series units were outlawed shortly after they were created.<ref name="Scene to Stats IG-Series" /> Several<ref name="IG-88C">{{FFGTCG|set=Technological Terror|cardname=IG-88C|link=opposition-cycle/technological-terror/ig-88c-r1936}}</ref><ref name="Card Game">{{FFGTCG|set=Desperate Circumstances|cardname=IG-88D|link=alliances-cycle/desperate-circumstances/ig-88d-r2032}}</ref> of them went on to work as [[bounty hunter]]s, including the infamous [[IG-88B]].<ref name="The Empire Strikes Back">{{Film|V}}</ref>

==History==
===Republic Era===
[[File:MagnaGuard-Utapau.jpg|left|thumb|290x290px|The MagnaGuards were one of Holowan's earliest successes]]
During the [[Republic Era|time]] of the [[Galactic Republic]], [[Holowan Laboratories]] manufactured [[IG lancer droid]]s for the [[InterGalactic Banking Clan]]'s [[IG-series battle droid army|droid army]]<ref name="Rise" /> using [[Phlut Design Systems]]'s plans for their failed [[assassin droid]];<ref name="BR2D2">[[Star Wars: Build Your Own R2-D2 16|''Star Wars: Build Your Own R2-D2'' 16]]</ref> these fought alongside [[IG-227 Hailfire-class droid tank|IG-227 ''Hailfire''-class droid tanks]] under the command of the [[Muun officer corps]].<ref name="Rise" /> With the eruption of the [[Clone Wars]], the Clan's army was integrated to the collective [[Separatist Droid Army|Army]] of the [[Confederacy of Independent Systems]] and IG lancer droids served alongside [[B1-series battle droid]]s during the [[List of battles|conflict]].<ref name="Scum and Villany" /> The success of the lancer and Hailfire droids led the Laboratories to also be awarded a contract for the creation of an elite class of bodyguard droids for the Separatists' highest ranking officials including [[General]] [[Grievous]].<ref name="BDOWD">{{EncyclopediaCite|Battle Droids and Other Droids of War}}</ref>

The resulting [[IG-100 MagnaGuard]] would go on to serve alongside Grievous until the very end of the war, when they also escorted the Separatist leadership to their last trip to [[Mustafar]].<ref name="Episode III">{{Film|III}}</ref> Another specialized model created by Holowan for the Separatists was the [[IG-86 sentinel droid]], an experimental combat unit designed to be a better battle droid.<ref name="Force Collection">{{ForceCollection|IG-86|star=4}}</ref> They saw heavy use by the Banking Clan's [[Collections and Security Division]] but were otherwise not as widespread as the B1s who retained their status as the Separatists' standard battle droid.<ref name="Obi-Wan 3">[[Obi-Wan 3|''Obi-Wan'' 3]]</ref> They were, however, very frequently used by third parties<ref name="Downfall">{{TCW|Downfall of a Droid}}</ref> including [[bounty hunter]]s such as [[Cad Bane]]<ref name="HC">{{TCW|Hostage Crisis}}</ref> as well as other criminals.<ref name="Downfall">{{TCW|Downfall of a Droid}}</ref>

===Imperial Era===
[[File:MosEisleyCantinaDispenser-SW100Objects.png|thumb|180px|A dispenser in Chalmun's Cantina made from the hollowed-out head of an IG unit]]
Following the end of the War and the defeat of the Separatists which saw the [[Proclamation of the New Order|rise]] of the [[Galactic Empire]], Holowan Laboratories, inspired by their wartime successes decided to begin the development of new types of even more evolved and specialized droids.<ref name="Scene to Stats IG-Series" /> One of the earliest models they created was the [[IG-RM bodyguard and enforcer droid]] which they designed to be as intimidating and scary-looking as possible; the unit became a staple among [[smuggler]]s and criminals of all kinds,<ref name="Star Wars (2015) 17">[[Star Wars (2015) 17|''Star Wars'' (2015) 17]]</ref> to the point where they were also known as "thug droids".<ref name="Spark of Rebellion">''[[Star Wars Rebels: Spark of Rebellion]]''</ref> The Holowan's next and presumably final attempt at a superior battle model was the [[IG-88 assassin droid]],<ref name="Ultimate Star Wars" /> nicknamed "Phlutoids" as a tribute to the inspiration behind the Holowan's first success.<ref name="BR2D2" /> These were a set of five identical, highly advanced [[assassin droid]]s which [[Holowan Laboratories massacre|slaughtered]] their [[Scientist|creators]] as soon as they were activated and destroyed the [[Unidentified laboratories (Holowan Laboratories)|laboratories]] they were contained in.<ref name="CVD">''[[Star Wars: The Complete Visual Dictionary, New Edition]]''</ref>

Two of the five droids, designated [[IG-88C]]<ref name="IG-88C">{{FFGTCG|set=Technological Terror|cardname=IG-88C|link=opposition-cycle/technological-terror/ig-88c-r1936}}</ref> and [[IG-88D]] would go on to roam the [[galaxy]] as bounty hunters<ref name="Card Game IG-88D">{{FFGTCG|set=Desperate Circumstances|cardname=IG-88D|link=https://www.rebelscum.com/alliances-cycle/desperate-circumstances/ig-88d-r2032}}</ref>, while another, [[IG-88B]] would go on to independently reprogram himself becoming one of the most famous bounty hunters of his [[Imperial Era|time]]<ref name="Sideshow">{{SideshowCite|set=Sideshow Collectibles|text=Sixth Scale Figures|pack=IG-88|altlink=https://www.sideshowtoy.com/collectibles/star-wars-ig-88-sideshow-collectibles-1000292}}</ref> and being one of the six hunters invited by [[Sith Lord]] [[Anakin Skywalker|Darth Vader]] to search for his son [[Luke Skywalker]]'s associate, [[Han Solo]].<ref name="The Empire Strikes Back">{{Film|V}}</ref> The droid designated [[IG-88A]] would go on to [[IG-88A's plan to overthrow organic life|plan]] to overthrow organic life.<ref name="HC2">{{HelmetCollectionCite|2|Weapons & Uniforms}}</ref>

[[Chalmun's Spaceport Cantina|Chalmun's Cantina]] in [[Mos Eisley]] had at least one dispenser above the bar that was made from the hollowed-out head of an old IG-series droid.<ref name="100 Objects">''[[Star Wars 100 Objects]]''</ref>

===New Republic Era===
[[File:IG12_MandoS3.png|thumb|left|200px|IG-11 was transformed into the mech IG-12]]
By the time of the [[New Republic]], IG [[memory circuit]]s were no longer manufactured and were hard to find.<ref name="Chapter 18" /> New developments in [[droidsmith]]ing by the [[Anzellan]]s on the planet [[Nevarro]] allowed for the modification of an IG unit, [[IG-11]] after its destruction; the droid had its [[memory circuit]] removed and its [[Artificial intelligence]] deactivated. Reduced to its basic motor functions, the drone&mdash;now designated [[IG-12]]&mdash;was used as a mechsuit for the [[species|alien]] [[Din Grogu|Grogu]].<ref name="Chapter 23">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 23: The Spies}}</ref> Later, IG-11 was fully repaired using spare parts and assigned to serve as the [[marshal]] of Nevarro.<ref name="Chapter 24">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 24: The Return}}</ref>

===Miscellaneous droids===
{{Main|IG-series assassin droid}}
Apart from their larger series, Holowan Laboratories also produced a number of specialized or unique models of [[IG-series assassin droid]]s; these included security droid [[BK-86]],<ref name="Traveler's Guide">''[[Star Wars: Galaxy's Edge: Traveler's Guide to Batuu]]''</ref> bounty hunter IG-11,<ref name="Chapter 1" /> enforcer [[IG-70]]<ref name="The Steal">''[[Adventures in Wild Space: The Steal]]''</ref> and small-time bounty hunter [[IG-90]].<ref name="Darth Vader 8">[[Darth Vader (2015) 8|''Darth Vader'' (2015) 8]]</ref> IG-11 was also modified to a custom-made [[mech]], [[IG-12]] by [[Anzellan Droidsmiths]] and used to carry [[Mandalorian]] [[foundling]] Din Grogu around.<ref name="Chapter 23" />

==Characteristics==
[[File:ArmedToTheTeeth-SoH.png|thumb|250x250px|IG-88B, an IG-series assassin with a conical head and claw-like hands]]
Droids of the IG-series were [[humanoid]], with conical heads and grasping claws in the place of hands.<ref name="Rise" /> They were designed as [[class four droid]]s with an emphasis on combat-focused upgrades, including [[acid]]-proof [[Servomotor|servo]] wires,<ref name="Updated and Expanded">''[[Star Wars Character Encyclopedia: Updated and Expanded]]''</ref> a variety of [[sensor]]s to detect movement, heat and sound<ref name="Droid Factory">[[Star Wars: Droid Factory (book)|''Star Wars: Droid Factory'']]</ref> as well as damage resistant [[armor]].<ref name="Updated and Expanded" /> Most of these droids were designed to be very agile to better combat enemies in close-quarters range;<ref name="Downfall" /> others, like the IG-RM droids were slower but more powerful.<ref name="Spark of Rebellion" /> They were extremely dangerous even when nearly destroyed; an IG-11 droid was able to attack and overwhelm a [[Mandalorian]] bounty hunter in full [[beskar]] [[armor]] while lacking its legs and arm and with its torso severely damaged.<ref name="Apostate">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 17: The Apostate}}</ref>

In order to protect Holowan's corporate secrets, at least the IG assassin units were hard-coded to destroy themselves with a built-in [[self-destruct mechanism]] which couldn't be overwritten by even manumitted droids (although they still retained the freedom to override the mechanism if there was a chance of escape).<ref name="Mandalorian">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 1: The Mandalorian}}</ref> Despite its nature, the mechanism wouldn't obliterate the droid, but rather destroy its most sensitive components such as the [[droid brain]] and chassis beyond repair.<ref name="Chapter 23" />

